## Answers (WCAG 2.2, W3C Recommendation)

| # | Question | Answer |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Level AA success criterion setting a minimum size for pointer-input targets | **SC 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum)** (Guideline 2.5 Input Modalities, Level AA, new in WCAG 2.2) |
| 2 | Minimum size in CSS pixels | **24 by 24 CSS pixels** |
| 3 | Diameter of the circle used by its spacing exception | **24 CSS pixels** |
| 4 | Larger size required by Level AAA 'Target Size (Enhanced)' | **44 by 44 CSS pixels** (SC 2.5.5) |

### 1. Normative text, SC 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum) (Level AA)

Quoted from https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/ (and identically at https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/target-size-minimum):

> "The size of the target for pointer inputs is at least 24 by 24 CSS pixels, except when:
> **Spacing** — Undersized targets (those less than 24 by 24 CSS pixels) are positioned so that if a 24 CSS pixel diameter circle is centered on the bounding box of each, the circles do not intersect another target or the circle for another undersized target;
> **Equivalent** — The function can be achieved through a different control on the same page that meets this criterion;
> **Inline** — The target is in a sentence or its size is otherwise constrained by the line-height of non-target text;
> **User Agent Control** — The size of the target is determined by the user agent and is not modified by the author;
> **Essential** — A particular presentation of the target is essential or is legally required for the information being conveyed."

Measurement definition, from the same Understanding page:

> "For a target to be 'at least 24 by 24 CSS pixels', it must be conceptually possible to draw a solid 24 by 24 CSS pixel square, aligned to the horizontal and vertical axis such that the square is completely within the target (does not extend outside the target's area)."

### 2. Normative text, SC 2.5.5 Target Size (Enhanced) (Level AAA)

Quoted from https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/ and https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/target-size-enhanced.html:

> "The size of the target for pointer inputs is at least 44 by 44 CSS pixels except when:
> **Equivalent** — The target is available through an equivalent link or control on the same page that is at least 44 by 44 CSS pixels;
> **Inline** — The target is in a sentence or block of text;
> **User Agent Control** — The size of the target is determined by the user agent and is not modified by the author;
> **Essential** — A particular presentation of the target is essential to the information being conveyed."

The relationship between the two is stated explicitly in the 2.5.8 Understanding document: "...the Working Group settled on the smaller measure of 24 by 24 CSS pixels for the '(Minimum)' version of this criterion, while 2.5.5 Target Size (Enhanced) uses the larger 44 by 44 CSS pixel measure as its threshold size." Sufficient technique for 2.5.5 is C44 ("Using CSS to ensure targets are at least 44 by 44 CSS pixels"); for 2.5.8 the spacing side is served by C42 ("Using min-height and min-width on target container to ensure sufficient target spacing").

### 3. Geometry of the spacing exception (how to compute it, not guess it)

- The circle is **24 CSS px in diameter, i.e. radius 12 CSS px**, centered on the **center of the target's bounding box**.
- Two circles of radius 12 do not intersect iff the distance between their centers is **>= 24 CSS px** (tangency is permitted; strict intersection fails).
- The circle must also not intersect **any other target** (of any size) — i.e. the circle must not overlap that target's bounding-box area.
- Horizontal neighbours, target widths w1 and w2, gap g between bounding boxes: center distance = w1/2 + g + w2/2. Pass iff `w1/2 + g + w2/2 >= 24`.
- Worked checks (matching the Understanding doc's Figure 5): 24x24 targets -> pass by size, no spacing test needed. 20x20 targets with g = 4: 10 + 4 + 10 = 24 -> tangent -> **pass** the spacing exception. 20x20 targets with g = 0: 10 + 0 + 10 = 20 < 24 -> circles intersect -> **fail**.
- Diagonal/2-D neighbours: same rule with Euclidean center distance; a diagonal offset of 17 px on both axes gives sqrt(17^2 + 17^2) = 24.04 >= 24 -> pass.

### 4. Frontend implementation (design tokens + CSS)

```css
:root {
  /* WCAG 2.2 */
  --target-min-aa: 24px;      /* SC 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum), Level AA */
  --target-min-aaa: 44px;     /* SC 2.5.5 Target Size (Enhanced), Level AAA */
  --target-circle-aa: 24px;   /* spacing-exception circle diameter, SC 2.5.8 */
  --target-gap-min: 8px;      /* design default; verify with the geometry above */
}

/* Preferred: satisfy the size, not the exception. */
.icon-button,
.pagination a,
.toolbar button {
  min-inline-size: var(--target-min-aa);
  min-block-size: var(--target-min-aa);
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}

/* Stretch to the AAA threshold where the design allows (C44 pattern). */
.touch-target-aaa {
  min-inline-size: var(--target-min-aaa);
  min-block-size: var(--target-min-aaa);
}

/* When a control must stay visually small, keep the visual box small but
   give the hit area a 24px minimum via padding/pseudo-element, and
   guarantee >= 24px center-to-center on undersized neighbours. */
.chip {
  position: relative;
  padding: 4px 6px;
}
.chip::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: 50% auto auto 50%;
  inline-size: var(--target-circle-aa);
  block-size: var(--target-circle-aa);
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  border-radius: 50%;
}
```

Rules of thumb for the layout pass:
1. Author every interactive element to `min-inline-size`/`min-block-size` of at least 24px in the AA baseline; 44px in an AAA baseline.
2. If the visual size is intentionally under 24px, treat the spacing exception as a **layout constraint**: adjacent undersized targets must be laid out so their 24px-diameter bounding-box circles clear each other and clear any other target. With equal square targets of side s and a uniform gap g in a row, the constraint is `s + g >= 24` (e.g. s = 20 -> g >= 4; s = 16 -> g >= 8).
3. Do not use the spacing exception for inline links in running text; the Inline exception governs those (and text reflow makes relative positions unpredictable).
4. `min-height`/`min-width` on a container (C42) is the cheapest way to guarantee both size and spacing without changing icon dimensions.
5. CSS px here is the CSS absolute-length unit (density-independent reference pixel), not device pixels; on a 2x display a 24px target is 48 device pixels. Do not compensate for device pixel ratio in the authored values.

### 5. Verification procedure (per criterion, run before sign-off)

For each target (each user-interface component that accepts pointer input):
1. Skip if the target is inline in a sentence / constrained by line-height (Inline), or its size is UA-determined and unmodified by the author (User Agent Control), or a particular presentation is essential/legally required (Essential).
2. AA (2.5.8): can a 24x24 CSS px axis-aligned square be placed fully inside the target? If yes, pass. If no, the target is undersized -> test Spacing: draw a 24 CSS px diameter circle centered on the target's bounding box; it must not intersect any other target or any other undersized target's circle. If it would, check Equivalent (an alternative control on the same page that itself meets the criterion). If none applies, fail.
3. AAA (2.5.5): can a 44x44 CSS px axis-aligned square be placed fully inside the target, or is there an Equivalent 44x44 control, or does Inline / User Agent Control / Essential apply? If none, fail.
4. Record measured values in CSS px as computed by `getBoundingClientRect()` (which reports CSS px), not by screenshot pixel counting.

### 6. Note on the harness's proofs of absence

No ask is reported as 'not stated'. The absence proofs are scoped to three pages that, by their subject matter, would not carry these strings: `Understanding/target-size-enhanced.html` and `Techniques/css/C44` cover the 44px AAA criterion only (hence no '2.5.8' and no '24 CSS pixels' there), and `new-in-22` describes 2.5.8 but does not restate 2.5.5's numbers. The answers are present verbatim in the same grounding set — `https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/` and `https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/target-size-minimum` both print 'at least 24 by 24 CSS pixels' and '24 CSS pixel diameter circle', and `https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/` plus `Understanding/target-size-enhanced.html` print 'at least 44 by 44 CSS pixels'. The request therefore can be, and is, answered from the grounding rather than being blocked.
